<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gyges of Lydia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 680–644 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gyges was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> from around 680 BCE and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mermnad-dynasty">Mermnad dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> tells how he killed the last Heraclid king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candaules">Candaules</a>, and took his throne and queen. He attacked the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, sent rich gifts to Delphi and sought Assyrian help against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>, who killed him around 644 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gyges-of-lydia.jpg" alt="Gyges of Lydia" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Der_Ring_des_Gyges_(Ferrara_16_Jh).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nothing is known of Gyges's origins except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>'s claim that he was the son of a man named Dascylus. His name is the Greek form Gugēs of the Lydian Kukas, which means grandfather, and the annals of the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> refer several times to Gu(g)gu, king of Luddi, who is identified with him. He is regarded as the first known Lydian king to attempt to turn the kingdom into a powerful empire.</p><p>He seems to have taken the throne by overthrowing King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candaules">Candaules</a> of the Heraclid dynasty, helped by a Carian prince from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milas">Mylasa</a> named Arselis. His rise came in a time of turmoil after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>, nomads from the Eurasian Steppe, destroyed the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, the previous major power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, around 675 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Immediately after Gyges seized the throne, the oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at Delphi confirmed his kingship, and he thanked it with lavish offerings of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, including six gold crates weighing thirty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> together. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, who saw them there, called them the Gygadas. Gyges attacked the Ionian cities of Miletus, Smyrna and Colophon. He could not take Miletus or Smyrna and made peace with both, granting the Milesians the right to found colonies on Lydian coasts; he seized only the lower city of Colophon. In the south he kept alliances with Carian dynasts and, according to Herodotus's account of the length of his reign, ruled for 38 years.</p><p>In 665 BCE he faced a war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>. Neo-Assyrian records say that the god Aššur appeared to him in a dream and told him to seek help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>. Gyges sent envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, but offered presents rather than tribute and so did not become an Assyrian vassal. He defeated the Cimmerians without Assyrian help and sent captured Cimmerian soldiers to Nineveh. Afterwards he took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troad">Troad</a> without much resistance, settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a> there and made a hunting reserve at Cyzicus. Around 662 BCE he established contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-i">Psamtik I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and until 658 BCE sent him Carian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greek</a> soldiers who helped Psamtik unite Egypt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> invaded again in 657 BCE, which Assyrian oracular reports call a bad omen for the Westland. In 644 BCE a third attack, led by their king Lygdamis, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a>, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> and killed Gyges. Assyrian records blamed his death on his decision to act apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, which they took for arrogance. His son Ardys succeeded him and resumed diplomacy with Assyria. His great-grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alyattes">Alyattes</a> put the legend Kukalim, meaning I am of Kukas, on coins, declaring that he belonged to the dynasty of Gyges.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gyges of Lydia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom becomes historical with <b>Gyges</b>, who seized the throne around 680 BCE by killing the last Heraclid king, Candaules; Herodotus tells a famous tale of how Candaules’ wife made Gyges choose between death and regicide.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The word tyrannos was probably borrowed: it may have come from Lydian or another language of Asia Minor, and one of its earliest uses is by the poet Archilochus, writing about <b>Gyges of Lydia</b>, who had killed King Candaules and taken his throne and his queen.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Settlers from Miletus founded Abydos c. 680 BCE on the Asian shore of the Hellespont, at the narrows opposite Sestos, with the agreement of the Lydian king <b>Gyges</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/abydos">Abydos</a></li><li><blockquote>They overran Phrygia around 696 BCE, when King Midas is said to have killed himself, and later sacked Sardis, killing <b>Gyges of Lydia</b> around 644 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gyges</b> of the Kingdom of Lydia captured its lower town in the 7th century BCE, and it then passed to the Achaemenid Empire and the Delian League.</blockquote><a href="/state/colophon">Colophon</a></li><li><blockquote>Smyrna fought the Kingdom of Lydia, resisting <b>Gyges</b>, but Alyattes destroyed it c. 600 BCE, after which it survived only as villages.</blockquote><a href="/state/smyrna">Smyrna</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.49,28.04&amp;year=-662" aria-label="Sardis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5247 782 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Gyges of Lydia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5697 1052h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5697 1052h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=38.49,28.04&amp;year=-662">What was here in 662 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ardys-of-lydia"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Ardys of Lydia</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 7th century BCE</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Our fathers, in time past, distinguished right and wrong plainly enough, and it is our wisdom to submit to be taught by them.&#8221;</p><footer>Refusing the king Candaules, who wished him to see the queen unclothed (Herodotus, Histories, book 1) &#183; tr. George Rawlinson, 1858 &#183; Herodotus's account &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_Herodotus_(Rawlinson)/Book_1" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
